Here's a rephrased, clearer version of that requirement:

EOD Snapshot Load Logic — Missing Row Type Recovery

Current behavior
The EOD snapshot load process reads data from the hourly snapshot and loads it into the EOD table, driven by a business-date parameter (SOD). Each run is designed to process the current day's data and move on.

The problem (edge case)
There's no guarantee that all instrument row types get fully loaded on a given business day. For example, if the Sept 1 run only captures a subset of row types (due to a delay, partial data availability, or failure), the process currently has no way to know this happened — and no mechanism to go back and pick up what was missed. As a result, those unloaded row types could be silently skipped forever, since the next day's run only looks at that day's data, not backfill from the prior day.

Why it matters
Every row type must eventually be captured for every business day — none can be permanently dropped just because a run happened to be incomplete.

Proposed solution
Introduce a checkpoint/status table in Oracle (not Hive) that tracks completeness per business day:

Flag = Y → all row types for that business day were successfully loaded. Nothing more to do for that date.
Flag = N → the load was incomplete for that business day. On the next run, the job should detect this and retry/backfill the missing row types for that date before or alongside processing the new day's data.

Net effect
This turns the load process from a simple "process today only" model into a resumable, self-healing model — each run checks not just today's data, but also whether any prior day was left incomplete, and closes that gap automatically.

